<section id="S6.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.1.2 </span>Stochastic Policy</h4>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The experiment in the stochastic policy environment was conducted in the same manner as the experiment in the deterministic policy environment.
We generated 1000 episodes and used the same transition probabilities in Figure <a href="#S5.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 5 Problem Setup ‣ Detecting Model Drifts in Non-Stationary Environment Using Edit Operation Measures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
The noise values were varied from 0 (no model drift) to 0.4 in increments of 0.1.
Table <a href="#S6.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ 6.1.2 Stochastic Policy ‣ 6.1 Maze Problem ‣ 6 Experimental Results ‣ Detecting Model Drifts in Non-Stationary Environment Using Edit Operation Measures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> and Table <a href="#S6.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 6.1.2 Stochastic Policy ‣ 6.1 Maze Problem ‣ 6 Experimental Results ‣ Detecting Model Drifts in Non-Stationary Environment Using Edit Operation Measures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> present the mean, standard deviation, and Welch’s t-test p-value for each measure across the noise levels. An asterisk (*) indicates statistically significant drift detection.</p>
</div>
